in @prism
in @kintsugi/fracture

# @kintsugi/fracture/refract-to-fixed — the second fracture rule.
#
# The drift: `refract <name> = <combinator>` appearing at module-top
# binding position. Per the 2026-05-26 8-category corpus audit
# (Cat 7), `refract` was overloaded — the Prism operation, the
# substrate keyword, AND the binding form for named combinators
# inside a grammar block. The triple-meaning was drift: the
# binding form is its own shape (a settled value with no holes,
# no inference) and deserves its own keyword.
#
# The canonical: `fixed <name> = <combinator>`. Per Alex 2026-05-26:
# "`fixed`. Like `const`. But math. And a `fixed` value cannot
# include any holes. No inference. It needs to be a fully settled
# crystal. Becomes content addressed." The binding IS the crystal
# IS the OID. Distinct from `beam` (body-altitude, dynamic, may
# carry Beam<T> metadata). The keyword itself is declared in the
# substrate as `type fixed = refract` — a typed alias for the
# refract Prism op at the binding altitude.
#
# Confidence = 1.0. The migration is unambiguous: every
# `refract <name> = <combinator>` line inside a grammar block
# becomes `fixed <name> = <combinator>`. The `<op> <keyword>`
# 2-word lines (e.g. `focus grammar`, `refract refract`) are NOT
# bindings and are left untouched — they declare keyword
# membership in the lexical table, not value bindings.
#
# Per @kintsugi/fracture: this declaration follows the FP collapse
# — one function with two laws. Detection IS asking whether the
# function is a no-op on the input.
#
# Spec: docs/specs/mirror-grammar-self-hosted.md
# Insight: docs/insights/2026-05-26-fixed-and-the-spectral-feedback-fracture.md

grammar @kintsugi/fracture/refract-to-fixed {
  # The fracture function. Walks the ast; rewrites every
  # `refract <name> = <combinator>` binding form to
  # `fixed <name> = <combinator>`. Preserves all other bytes.
  # The `<op> <keyword>` lines that declare keyword membership
  # are NOT bindings and are structurally distinct (no `=` at the
  # binding altitude) — they are left untouched. The body is a
  # Fate-resolved hole; the actual rewrite logic lives at the
  # downstream evaluator altitude. The substrate-pull discipline
  # says: declare the function + the laws; the implementation is
  # downstream.
  flow(ast) -> ast { \ }

  # Law 1: idempotent. Once `refract <name> = <combinator>` has
  # been migrated to `fixed <name> = <combinator>`, running flow
  # again is a no-op — there are no further refract-bindings to
  # find. The canonical corpus is a fixed point. Bound via
  # @kintsugi/fracture.
  requires idempotent(flow)

  # Law 2: canonical at fixpoint. flow(x) == x exactly when x has
  # no refract-at-binding-position drift. This IS the detection:
  # a file is canonical iff the fracture is a no-op on it. No
  # separate detect operation.
  requires canonical_at_fixpoint(flow)
}

out flow
